    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Knossos grew from the ruins of the Minoan palace into one of the two leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>.</li><li>It was governed by annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosmoi">kosmoi</a> and a council of elders, with men's messes and age groups for citizen training.</li><li>Long rivalry with Gortyn and Lyttos ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 69–67 BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Classical Knossos put the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a> on its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, sometimes with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>, centuries after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> palace that inspired the legend had fallen into ruin.</p></aside>
    </section>

