    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/minos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minos.jpg" alt="Minos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syriskos_Painter_-_ARV_259_1_-_Theseus_killing_the_Minotauros_in_the_presence_of_Minos_and_Ariadne_and_lookers_-_Athens_NAM_15115.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ArchaiOptix, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Knossos lies a few kilometres inland from the north coast of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairatos">Kairatos</a> stream. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> palace, the largest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoan Crete</a>, was destroyed in the fourteenth or thirteenth century BCE (the date of its final destruction is still debated), but the site was never abandoned. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> a substantial town grew up around the ruins, and the rich tombs of the North Cemetery, used from the eleventh to the seventh century BCE, contain bronzes, gold jewellery and pottery imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, showing that Knossos was one of the most prosperous and outward-looking communities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> around 900–700 BCE. By the Archaic period its inhabitants spoke the Doric dialect common to most of Crete, and Knossos had become a polis with its own territory, institutions and port at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amnisos">Amnisos</a> and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Herakleion</a>, the site of modern Heraklion. Greek memory linked the city to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Age past: the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a></em> names Knossos first among the Cretan cities led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idomeneus">Idomeneus</a>, and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a></em> calls it the great city where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a> ruled. Like much of Crete, Knossos is archaeologically less visible in the sixth century BCE, but it re-emerged in the Classical period as, with Gortyn, one of the two strongest cities of the island.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heraklion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heraklion.jpg" alt="Heraklion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraklion_Montage_L.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SilentResident (compilation, sources to attribute see above; if any of the sources used in the Montage are removed fr..., CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Knossian society followed the pattern ancient writers described for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> as a whole. Full citizens formed a privileged class, while the land was worked by dependent rural populations, called by various names in Cretan sources, who were bound to the soil and excluded from political life; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> compared them to the helots of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, though he noted they were less rebellious. Citizen men ate together in men's houses, the <em>andreia</em>, whose meals Aristotle says were funded from public produce, and boys were trained in groups called <em>agelai</em>. The economy rested on cereals, olives, vines and stock-raising in the territory between the coast and the uplands, with trade through the harbours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amnisos">Amnisos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Herakleion</a>. From the later fifth century Knossos struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins showing a stylised labyrinth on one side and often the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a> or a head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> on the other, an explicit claim on the Minoan legends. Excavations have revealed a sanctuary of Demeter in use from the eighth century BCE into Roman times. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Cretan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, served across the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and piracy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slave-raiding">slave raiding</a> became a significant, and notorious, part of the island's economy, in which Knossos shared.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polybius.jpg" alt="Polybius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_des_Polybios.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, Livius Onderwijs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like other Cretan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a>, Knossos fielded a citizen army trained through the <em>agelai</em> and men's houses, and Cretans were famed above all as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and light troops. Warfare on the island was endemic, as the cities competed for land, harbours and allies, and many interstate treaties survive as inscriptions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Knossos and Gortyn, sometimes allied and sometimes enemies, sought to dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, by the late third century they had brought most of the island under their control, with the exception of Lyttos. In the Lyttian War of about 221–219 BCE Knossos, supported by Aetolian troops, attacked Lyttos, and while the Lyttian army was away on campaign the Knossians seized the city and destroyed it around 220 BCE. The war split Gortyn into rival factions and drew in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> and the Achaean League on the side of Knossos' opponents. Through the second century Knossos and Gortyn alternately cooperated and fought, while Cretan cities clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in the Cretan War of about 205–200 BCE. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> turned on Crete, the Cretans defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-antonius-creticus">Marcus Antonius</a> at sea near Kydonia around 71 BCE, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus</a> then reduced the cities one by one, including Knossos, between 69 and 67 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Knossos was governed by the institutions shared by the Cretan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a>. Executive power lay with a board of annual magistrates, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosmoi">kosmoi</a></em>, ten in number according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, drawn from certain privileged kinship groups and led by a chief kosmos who gave his name to the year. Former kosmoi formed a council of elders, while the citizen assembly had limited powers, chiefly to ratify decisions taken by the magistrates and council. Aristotle, in the <em>Politics</em>, criticised this system for its narrow recruitment and the habit of suspending the kosmoi during factional struggles. Knossos conducted its foreign affairs through sworn treaties; an inscription of about 450 BCE records an agreement between Knossos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tylissos">Tylissos</a> made under the arbitration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Knossos concluded treaties of alliance and shared citizenship with many Cretan cities. The young men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dreros">Dreros</a> swore an oath, preserved on stone, to be hostile to Lyttos and friendly to Knossos. In the late third century the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> formed a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cretan-koinon">Cretan Koinon</a> with a common court for disputes between cities, in which Knossos was a leading member, and Philip V was recognised as its protector around 216 BCE. Its cohesion was weak, and the Koinon repeatedly broke apart in local wars.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/athens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="Athens" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Parlette, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Knossos' chief legacy lies in the way it carried the memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> into Greek culture. The myths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a>, the lawgiver king of Knossos, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>, circulated throughout the Greek world, and the city deliberately advertised them on its coinage. The seer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epimenides">Epimenides</a>, traditionally said to be a Knossian, was remembered for purifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> after the sacrilege of the Cylonian conspiracy, and a line attributed to him, that Cretans are always liars, is quoted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> letter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a>. Cretan institutions fascinated political thinkers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephorus">Ephorus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> compared them with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> set his last dialogue, the <em>Laws</em>, among three old men walking from Knossos to the cave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ida-crete">Mount Ida</a>, one of them a Knossian named Kleinias who is charged with founding a new Cretan city. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> Knossos became the colony of Colonia Iulia Nobilis Cnosus and later a bishopric. Its greatest modern impact came from archaeology: excavation of the Minoan palace by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> from 1900 revealed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> Age civilisation he named Minoan, while later work by the British School at Athens has made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> Knossos one of the best known Greek towns of its era.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rome.jpg" alt="Rome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trevi_Fountain,_Rome,_Italy_2_-_May_2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diliff, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of independent Knossos was tied to the fate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> within the expanding Roman world. Endless wars among the Cretan cities, competition between Knossos and Gortyn, and the island's reputation as a base for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and slavers made Crete a target for Roman intervention, especially once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> undertook to suppress piracy in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Cretan support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> and refusal of Roman demands to surrender pirate leaders and hostages led to war. After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-antonius-creticus">Marcus Antonius</a> around 71 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-creticus">Quintus Caecilius Metellus</a> with three legions in 69 BCE. He defeated the Cretans near Kydonia and then besieged the cities in turn; at Knossos the Cretan commander Lasthenes held out before escaping, and by 67 BCE the island was conquered, a campaign that earned Metellus the surname Creticus. Crete became a Roman province, later combined with Cyrene, with its capital at Gortyn, Knossos' old rival. Knossos lost its independence and much of its importance, but was refounded as a Roman colony, probably under Octavian or Augustus in the late first century BCE, and continued as a prosperous provincial town through the Roman period.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Knossos matters as the city that bridged Minoan and Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>: built over the greatest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> palace, it became one of the island's two leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">poleis</a>, kept the legends of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a> alive on its coins, shaped Greek ideas of Cretan law, and fought Gortyn and Lyttos until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s conquest in 67 BCE.</em></p>

